## Step 1 — Verified Critical AD Services

Opened PowerShell as Administrator on DC01 and ran:

```powershell
Get-Service adws,kdc,netlogon,dns
```

All four services confirmed **Running:**

| Service | Full Name | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `adws` | Active Directory Web Services | Allows PowerShell AD module to communicate with AD |
| `kdc` | Kerberos Key Distribution Center | Handles all domain authentication requests |
| `netlogon` | Netlogon | Processes domain logon requests from client machines |
| `dns` | DNS Server | Resolves domain names — backbone of AD functionality |

**Why this matters:** If any of these services stop, the domain stops functioning. NETLOGON down means no one can log in. KDC down means Kerberos authentication fails across the board. DNS down means clients can't locate the DC at all. These four are the first thing to check on any DC health call.

---

## Step 2 — Verified Domain and DC Role

```powershell
Get-ADDomain
```

**Key output confirmed:**
- `DNSRoot` — `homelab.local`
- `PDCEmulator` — `DC01.homelab.local`
- `RIDMaster` — `DC01.homelab.local`
- `InfrastructureRole` — `DC01.homelab.local`

All five FSMO (Flexible Single Master Operations) roles held by DC01, as expected in a single-DC forest.

```powershell
Get-ADDomainController
```

Confirmed DC01 recognized as domain controller with correct IP, OS version, and site assignment.

**What FSMO roles are:** In an AD forest, certain sensitive operations can only be performed by one DC at a time to prevent conflicts — things like creating new security identifiers for objects, managing password changes, and keeping time synchronized across the domain. These are called FSMO roles. In a single-DC lab, one server holds all five. In larger environments they can be distributed across multiple DCs.

