## Step 8 — DC Diagnostics (dcdiag)

```powershell
dcdiag /v > C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\dcdiag-results.txt
```

Output redirected to text file to avoid BSOD triggers from clipboard operations in PowerShell (see BSOD section). Results reviewed from Notepad.

**All tests passed successfully**, including:

| Test | Result |
|---|---|
| Connectivity | Passed |
| Replications | Passed |
| NCSecDesc | Passed |
| NetLogons | Passed |
| Advertising | Passed |
| KnowsOfRoleHolders | Passed |
| RidManager | Passed |
| MachineAccount | Passed |
| Services | Passed |
| DFSREvent | Passed |

**What dcdiag is:** The standard Microsoft diagnostic tool for verifying domain controller health. It runs a full battery of tests covering connectivity, replication, security descriptors, service status, FSMO role recognition, and more. Running `dcdiag /v` after any major DC operation is standard sysadmin practice — if something is wrong with your DC, dcdiag will usually tell you what and where.

