## Lessons Learned

- `dcdiag /v` is the standard post-promotion health check for any domain controller — all tests passing confirms AD DS, DNS, NETLOGON, SYSVOL, and replication are functioning correctly
- Redirecting long PowerShell outputs to text files (`command > file.txt`) is cleaner and more reliable than copying from the terminal window — standard sysadmin practice
- FSMO roles are critical AD functions that only one DC can perform at a time — in a single-DC lab all five roles are held by the same server
- The `_msdcs` DNS records are automatically created during DC promotion and are what AD clients use to locate domain services — their presence is a health indicator
- SYSVOL and NETLOGON share existence should always be verified after DC promotion — missing shares indicate an incomplete promotion
- OU structure should be established before users and computers are created — moving objects after the fact works but applying Group Policy retroactively is more complex
- A `DistinguishedName` in AD reads right to left and describes the full directory path to an object — `CN=WIN11-01,CN=HOMELAB_COMPUTERS,DC=homelab,DC=local` means WIN11-01 is in HOMELAB_COMPUTERS in the homelab.local domain
- VMware status bar messages can provide critical diagnostic clues during VM instability — the "Cannot connect to floppy A" message directly identified the BSOD root cause
- When a VM shows escalating stop codes (new codes appearing alongside recurring ones), treat it as an urgent stability issue rather than a one-time spike

